DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     lldd combines relocatable object files, produced by cccc,  aass,
     or  lldd, with routines drawn from library archives produced
     by aarr, to build an executable (or further relocatable) out-
     put file.

     lldd scans its arguments left to right.  Each non-option argu-
     ment is either a relocatable object file or a library ar-
     chive.  A library archive is searched only to resolve un-
     defined references; if there are none outstanding, lldd goes
     on to the next argument immediately.  The library is scanned
     from first module to last, repeatedly, until a whole pass
     adds nothing further to the output; a module is bound in as
     soon as it resolves an outstanding undefined symbol.

     The following options are recognized:

     -dd   Define common symbols in the output even if undefined
          symbols remain.  Normally commons are defined only when
          the link is otherwise complete (no undefined symbols,
          or relocatable output).

     -ee _e_n_t
          Set the entry point of the output to _e_n_t, given either
          as a symbol name or as a number (a leading 00 means
          octal, 00xx means hexadecimal).

     -ii   Bind the output with separated (sepid) text and data.

     -kk[_s_y_s]
          Link a kernel process or loadable driver: read the sym-
          bol table of _s_y_s (default /ccoohheerreenntt) to resolve
          references against the running kernel, and mark the
          output as a kernel image.

     -ll_l_i_b
          An abbreviation for the library /lliibb/lliibb_l_i_b.aa, or
          /uussrr/lliibb/lliibb_l_i_b.aa if the first is not found.

     -LL   Build large-model output: relax the check that a seg-
          ment's address range fits within the machine's normal
          per-segment size limit.

     -mm   Build the output image in memory, when it is small
          enough to fit, instead of seeking a disk file for each
          segment.  Larger memory use, faster link.

     -nn   Bind the output as a shared (pure) text image.



